## Abstract
For phishing URL detection, many approaches based on various machine learning methods suggest using the URL string, webpage content, and additional information; also, there are studies that analyze the robustness of the generated or modified URLs and the failure of detection. In this review, we will classify related research into detection, synthetic URL generation, robustness, and diagnosis, and compare each approach's input information, experimental role, and limitations. Based on this literature, our project aims to perform E3 diagnosis in a generated-input E2 setting. Specifically, the fixed detector trained on real-world data is applied to the generated URL, and we analyze the relationship between URL features and detection and non-detection results. The intended contribution of our project is to specify, in an interpretable form, which URL features are related to non-detection, to expand the existing generated-input robustness evaluation into a more detailed outcome-conditioned diagnosis, rather than only measuring the degradation of detection performance for the generated URLs.

## Positioning Our project:
Among existing phishing URL research, the approach most relevant to our project is E2, which evaluates existing detection robustness using generated or modified URLs, and E3, which analyzes features of URLs or the relationship between specific subgroups and detection results. The research by DeepPhish and Ahamed utilized generated or modified input data to evaluate the performance of existing detection, and the research by Mia and DivExplorer analyzed the relationship between the group defined by specific contribution or feature and the results of the model.

Our project combines these two directions to perform E3 diagnosis in a generated-input E2 setting. After we fix the Phishing URL detector trained on real-world data, we input the generated URL and analyze which URL feature is associated with different outcomes between detected and undetected URLs. As a result, our purpose is not only to show the fact that generated URLs could bypass existing detectors, but also to characterize, in an interpretable form, which features of generated URLs are related to the fixed detector's non-detection.

To this end, we will include URL-property condition, coverage, non-detection rate, comparison baseline, and uncertainty in each diagnosis result, and separate discovery and confirmation. Through this analysis, we will expand the existing generated-input robustness evaluation to detailed outcome-conditioned diagnosis, and will not interpret the observed relationship as a causal relationship.